{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "openregister-working-calendars-v1",
    "name": "OpenRegister Working Calendars API",
    "description": "The working calendar over its PUBLIC api, which is the objects api (rows 8.12 and Q8.20).\n\nWHY THESE ARE CONTRACT TESTS\n\nThe register's note on 8.12 counted five private implementations of a working day in the fleet, four different definitions of one, and no settings screen anywhere. The engine half already existed: a calendar is an object in the flow-timers register and the timer store resolves it. What did not exist was the guarantee that a write arriving through the api is refused on the same grounds a write through the admin page is.\n\nSo the assertions below are mostly about refusals and about arithmetic nobody can tabulate. A calendar made only of enumerated dates has an expiry date and degrades silently after it, which is why it has to be a 422 rather than a stored row. Koningsdag 2031 is the other half: 27 April 2031 is a Sunday, so the observed date is the 26th, and a hand-written table gets that right only for the years somebody thought about.\n\nThere is deliberately no calendar CRUD endpoint to test. The objects api is the api.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
  "auth": {
    "type": "basic",
    "basic": [
      {
        "key": "username",
        "value": "{{username}}",
        "type": "string"
      },
      {
        "key": "password",
        "value": "{{password}}",
        "type": "string"
      }
    ]
  },
  "event": [
    {
      "listen": "prerequest",
      "script": {
        "type": "text/javascript",
        "exec": [
          "if (!pm.collectionVariables.get('calendarTimestamp')) {",
          "    pm.collectionVariables.set('calendarTimestamp', String(Date.now()));",
          "}"
        ]
      }
    }
